K. L. Saigal died on 18 January 1947 and the film was released several months following his death. Saigal had become addicted to alcohol and, according to medical advisors of the time, he could record only after drinking. But for Parwana, he recorded the songs while refraining from taking alcohol. According to composer Naushad, he had recorded the song "Jab Dil Hi Toot Gaya" for Shah Jehan (1946) after asking Saigal to sing without having alcohol, which Saigal did after some reluctance and uncertainty on his part.
The news spread and Khurshid Anwar then got Saigal to do the same with the songs in Parwana. The music became very popular. Saigal's songs had personified the musical era of the 1930s and 1940s, which was also known as the Saigal Era.
